  "claim": "The delay a type prints `FL Dly` against and the loop it prints `FL Fb` against are, in both types that print them inside a larger chain, the delay and the loop of the stereo flanger that stands alone. Read as a comb at two settings of the delay byte and on both signs of the loop, all four readings of each type return the single type's figures exactly: 0.618 ms at a delay byte of 6 and 1.318 ms at 13 with the loop in phase, 0.624 and 1.296 with it inverted. That holds where the chorus runs into the flanger and where it sits beside it, so neither arrangement carries a delay line of its own. The teeth are 22.58 dB deep at the shallowest against the 16.05 dB the deepest of these records asks of one, and each profile scores as a comb of that spacing by at least 1.55 times what the same profile scores read on the other sign -- which is the control a filter sitting somewhere cannot imitate, since a peak scores the same either way.",
  "named": null,
  "adds": "The single type's claim says the byte is a loop and says what it is around. It says that on one type. These two print the same pair of bytes at the same addresses over the same printed ranges, and nothing structural had been read on either of them -- the only claims naming them were the two that run across every type, about what a parameter powers up holding and about what happens to a byte its page prints no state for. What is added is that the stage a combination names after another type is that type's stage, read on the one quantity a delay line cannot fake: where its comb sits.",
