      "ruled_out_by": "Two separately built delays would have to agree with the single type's and with each other at both settings, and the reading resolves them finely enough for that to be a claim: the fit returns 0.618 ms against 1.318 for a delay byte moving from 6 to 13, which is a ratio of 2.133 where the byte's own is 2.167. All eight readings across the two types land on the single type's two figures and on no other."
    },
    {
      "reading": "The teeth are a filter of the combination's own rather than a loop, and they happen to sit where the single type's do.",
      "standing": false,
      "ruled_out_by": "A comb and a peak are told apart by what happens below the printed nought of the feedback byte. Every profile was scored both as an in-phase comb and as an inverting one, and each of the eight scores at least 1.55 times better on the sign its setting implies -- the settings under the centre on the inverting reading and those over it on the in-phase one. A filter sitting somewhere scores the same both ways."
    },
    {
      "reading": "The chorus in front of or beside the flanger is still in the path, and what was read is the two stages together.",
      "standing": false,
      "ruled_out_by": "Each run wrote the chorus out of the path in the way its own arrangement allows -- the series type's chorus balance to its dry end, the parallel type's chorus level to nothing -- and parked its rate and depth as well. A modulated stage left in the path smears a comb into a shelf, which is what the first published reading of a modulated type came back as; these come back as combs of a single spacing."
    },
